# T56 Local sensitive-content response drill — amended pretrial plan (v4)

Date: 2026-10-15 UTC  
Implementation source checkpoint: session 1128 at `cc31a0de198dbd5231bbeebc9b78aa7a05cd6cf8` (tree `7d872ee2c3fdee8a5baabdcbdd2802dceb20ff3e`)  
Status: plan only; no local/manual composite trial or fixture creation has occurred in this session. The composite added at this checkpoint is an unignored `#[test]`, so automatic CI may run that prior v2-shaped test; preserve any such exact source-bound result separately and do not count it as a v4 trial. This v4 leaves v2 byte-exact (SHA-256 `f36642a8b3dc55b13d70970d4c696a87b9239f881bbc642a4f6e3d9fc54a950e`) and v3 byte-exact (SHA-256 `ee53ad7b76828f2c303fdf548598b57e190947bdea358ff5674d32f138926a43`). It narrows v3's interrupted-view cleanup rule: invalid pins never authorize deletion or automatic repair; retry after refusal is permitted only for a previously busy pin or unreleased hold after the fixture-owned blocker is released. Missing-pin terminal metadata cleanup is permitted only when the immutable inventory and durable item states prove all non-pin content is absent/deleted, `CURRENT` is absent, and no unknown entries remain. This v4 must receive independent review before any fixture creation or runtime trial. The compile-only broker check passed; no synthetic token was used.

## Scope and fixture

Use one newly created, disposable Playground repository and collaboration project under a unique `/private/tmp/t56-playground-<run-id>/` root. Do not use Aethyme, evaluation repositories, held-out data, a provider, E1/FX08, or any reused FX17 attempt. Generate one random synthetic token in memory. Put it only in a tracked fixture source file. Do not put its literal in test names, paths, command arguments, environment variables, status JSON, or logs. Reader probes emit only boolean results and opaque digests.

Seed the same token through the supported Local workflow so these copies exist before quarantine: source working tree; captured archive/CAS and capture receipt; indexed analysis view; prepared generation source checkout; import rehearsal source checkout; and an exported bundle at an explicit destination outside the managed project root. Use a new operation/record ID for every stage. The external export and original Playground worktree are expected unmanaged residuals. Any named view hold that has not been explicitly released—including a hold whose `until_ms` is in the past—or unreadable hold metadata requires retaining that entire view as an explicit retryable residual and keeping `managed_cleanup_complete` false. Post-quarantine hold release is permitted as a metadata-only operation; content reads remain blocked. A busy generation pin, missing pin, malformed/non-regular pin, or symlinked pin requires retaining the entire view if any inventoried content remains. Missing or unsafe pins are never recreated or treated as permission to delete content. After all holds are released and every generation pin is exclusively held, exact inventoried view content may be deleted under the protocol below. Use a separate no-view fresh seed only if an independent positive managed-database cleanup receipt case is needed.

## Pre-trial witness

Capture a hash-bound, content-free witness bundle (hashes establish byte identity only; they are not signatures or authentication) before each case: fixture commit/tree and source-file SHA-256/bytes; project key and schema/floor; authority state/epoch; capture/generation/import/view/export IDs; sorted exact relative managed file paths with SHA-256/bytes; logical SQLite table names, row counts, bytes, and content digests; external export SHA-256/bytes; and the exact old-v23 executable SHA-256. For `views/`, additionally freeze a type-aware immutable layout containing every view file and directory, including `CURRENT` and each `.pin`, and bind that layout into the inventory digest. File items carry exact type/hash/byte state; directory entries carry their exact relative path/type. Symlinks, non-regular entries, and unclassified paths are recorded as unknown and are never deletion candidates. Do not infer the original directory set from a post-crash scan. Probes confirm the token is present in intended source-backed copies, but persist only booleans. Preserve the literal only in the disposable fixture. Raw command output must not print file contents.

## Positive quarantine, ingress, and bounded purge

1. Create the synthetic fixture and copies listed above with the new binary. Close all writer handles, then retain one analysis-view reader and one named hold for the pin-residual case.
2. Freeze the project with the local authority flow. Quarantine the exact project under a fixed reason code and record the immutable request digest.
3. Immediately verify the durable marker and DB incident witness. New response barriers, capture, view build/open/query, generation load/prepare, import/rehearse, and export must all fail closed. Existing view reader metadata may remain available; its content queries must fail closed. Do not claim already-open legacy processes have been revoked.
4. Attempt purge while the reader/hold and unresolved/unknown-job witness remain. Require the unknown-job fail-closed outcome and unchanged view bytes. Resolve only the fixture-owned job; while the real reader and named hold remain, retry purge and require a quarantined/incomplete result, byte-identical view inventory, and a whole-view reader/hold residual. No managed cleanup completion is allowed in this phase. Synthetic unresolved/unknown-job unit witnesses test the fail-closed branch only; they do not prove behavior for an actual native dispatching job. Keep the separately source-bound #776 native-gate retention evidence as the actual dispatch-liveness evidence.
5. Release only the fixture-owned named hold through the metadata-only hold-release path and verify this succeeds while content reads remain blocked; then drop its actual `ViewReader`. Do not kill unrelated processes or claim to revoke already-open v23 readers. Resume purge using the original request digest. Under archive EX plus response EX, inspect the whole view tree and all hold metadata before the first unlink. Treat every unreleased named hold as live regardless of expiry; malformed or unreadable hold metadata is unknown and blocks deletion. The nonterminal layout must contain a regular, non-symlink `CURRENT` whose value parses strictly to exactly one inventoried `gen-*` directory; absent or malformed `CURRENT`, an empty/unknown layout, unclassified entries, and symlinks fail closed before unlink. For every inventoried `gen-*` child—including superseded generations—require an existing regular, non-symlink `.pin`; acquire nonblocking exclusive locks for all existing pins in deterministic path order and hold every lock through deletion. A missing pin is not repairable and does not authorize deleting remaining content. It can be accepted only for terminal metadata-only cleanup when the immutable type-aware view-layout inventory and durable per-item states prove that every non-pin file item across the whole view is deleted and absent, the original `CURRENT` item is deleted and absent, the exact original pin item is already durably recorded deleted, every remaining directory is an originally inventoried directory with no unclassified contents, and no unknown entries remain. A malformed, non-regular, symlinked, or unreadable pin always remains a refusal/residual; never recreate, replace, unlink, or otherwise repair it. Validate the complete path/type/hash inventory before the first unlink. If any hold is unreleased or unknown, any existing pin is busy, or any pin/layout state fails these rules, delete nothing from that whole view, record a residual, and keep managed completion false. Once preflight succeeds, delete only inventoried generation content while leaving every pin and `CURRENT` in place; sync and durably record each exact item state. Remove `CURRENT` only after all generations' content is durably deleted and absent, then durably record its exact deleted state. Unlink all existing exclusively locked `.pin` files last and durably record each exact pin state. Remove directories only after confirming they are empty and match the immutable directory inventory; remove only originally inventoried directories, sync affected directories, then release pin locks. On I/O failure, retain the remaining original-inventory partition and report incomplete. After a crash, absent-pin cleanup may finish only the exact terminal metadata-only partition defined above; a missing pin with pending/present content, `CURRENT` present, or any unknown path remains a refusal/residual. Likewise, an absent `CURRENT` in any nonterminal or unknown layout fails before unlink. Retry the same immutable request only for a previously busy pin or unreleased hold after that fixture-owned blocker is released; missing, malformed, symlinked, unreadable, or unknown layouts are refusal-only and are not automatically repaired. Exact inventoried CAS/archive, generation, and import-rehearsal paths may also be deleted only after path/type/hash revalidation and directory sync. The source worktree and exported bundle stay unchanged and are individually named as external residuals.
6. Reopen the store and recheck marker, incident, immutable inventory, per-table states, cleanup receipt, and status. Assert the view tree is absent after the successful post-reader purge; separately assert the held-reader case preserved identical view bytes. Report `managed_cleanup_complete` only when its exact managed-file and database predicates pass; report the incident as quarantined and list all remaining copies. Never call this global erasure or secret purging from the host.

## Separate disposable failure-boundary cases

Start each case from a fresh copy of the same seed witness; retain each raw log, structured status, before/after hashes, and source commit binding.

- **After durable marker, before incident row:** inject a stop at this boundary. Restart new code; all ingress and authority resume must refuse. Re-running quarantine with the same scope must recover the same incident, not create a second incident or rotate authority.
- **After inventory commit, before first deletion:** stop, restart, require the identical inventory digest and resume exact deletion without rescanning into a changed scope.
- **After one exact managed file deletion:** stop, restart, require the deleted/pending partition to match the witness and continue idempotently.
- **After last file deletion, before database cleanup receipt:** stop, require status not to claim managed completion; retry cleanup and verify receipt is written only after FK-safe table clearing, checkpoint/VACUUM, file and directory sync.
- **Database cleanup refusal/busy:** induce a deterministic SQLite busy/failure at the cleanup boundary. Require no cleanup receipt, no cleared-table claims, managed completion false, and `foreign_keys` restored after the error. Retry only after releasing the fixture-owned blocker.
- **After durable database cleanup receipt:** retry status/purge; require stable incident identity, stable inventory/receipt, and no second destructive pass.
- **View-pin refusal controls:** from independent fresh seeds, test (a) a missing `.pin` with content still present, (b) a directory or other malformed/non-regular `.pin` path, (c) a symlinked `.pin`, (d) a busy current-generation pin held by a real `ViewReader`, and (e) a busy superseded-generation pin held after publishing a newer `CURRENT`. Missing, malformed, and symlinked pin cases must refuse before the first unlink, preserve the whole view bytes, and remain refusal-only residuals; do not recreate/repair the pin or claim automatic retry. For busy current/superseded pins and unreleased holds, retain the view; after the fixture-owned reader/pin or named hold is explicitly released, retry the same immutable request and require successful deletion. Test a missing or malformed `CURRENT` on a nonterminal layout and require refusal before the first unlink. Also interrupt after the last generation content item is durably marked deleted and absent but before `CURRENT` removal, then after `CURRENT` removal but before pin cleanup, and after the last pin has been unlinked and its exact item state durably recorded but before empty-directory cleanup. Restart may proceed without a pin only if it proves the exact terminal partition: immutable type-aware inventory matches, every non-pin file item is durably deleted and absent, the original `CURRENT` item is durably deleted and absent, the exact original pin item is durably marked deleted if absent, all remaining directories are in the original inventory with no unclassified contents, and there are no unknown entries. A crash after unlink but before the pin's deletion state is durable remains a refusal/residual. Otherwise preserve the whole view as a refusal/residual. The old-v23 future-open fence does not revoke an already-open v23 reader; while its shared pin remains, that view stays as a busy-pin residual.

No global host-memory pressure, SIGKILL-as-OOM claim, production marker edits, or unsandboxed deletion is part of this drill.

## Old-v23 compatibility fence

Use the preserved old-v23 executable only against a disposable copy of the new schema/floor-24 Playground state, never against Aethyme or the active fixture. First hash the executable and copied DB/project tree. Invoke the old binary's read/open path (`aethyme collab authority status`) with the disposable project selected exactly as the isolated CLI fixture requires. Require the exact schema/floor diagnostic before mutation: `<disposable state.db path> has schema 24, readable only by binaries supporting schema 24 or later; this binary supports 23. Upgrade Aethyme`. This is the `schema_too_new` case with found=24, min_compatible=24, and old-binary support=23. Require the exact rendered diagnostic line as well as nonzero exit; another refusal is not credit. Capture stderr/status and byte-compare the DB and managed file inventory before/after. Preserve executable hash `sha256:1268402c5d49ac303b61bc28b4e4726bf95b1d17d64d7099850f2f67950f7e6f`; do not rebuild or substitute it. A refusal proves only future opens through that binary; it does not stop a v23 process/FD already open before upgrade, and an already-open v23 `ViewReader` can continue querying while its shared pin exists because it does not use the new response barrier.

## Ordinary tests and named neutral mutations

For every focused deterministic test and every mutation run, freeze the exact source tree SHA before execution, source-file SHA-256 manifest, test selector, command, raw stdout/stderr, exit status, and parsed test count. Mutation trials are separate one-change worktrees/copies. Before mutation, save byte-identical backups of every changed source file. Run one named test only; require the named assertion/failure and expected count. Restore each file from its raw backup and verify byte-for-byte equality plus the full source SHA. If restoration or status is missing, classify the attempt incomplete; never infer an exit.

Predeclared negative-oracle mutants:

- Remove only the tombstone rejection from one selected ingress; the named post-quarantine ingress refusal assertion must fail.
- Omit the `generations/` inventory root; the generation-copy retention assertion must fail with the exact remaining relative path/hash.
- Omit the `import-rehearsals/` inventory root; the import-copy retention assertion must fail with the exact remaining relative path/hash.
- Set the database cleanup receipt before checkpoint/VACUUM/sync; the last-file-before-DB-cleanup boundary must reject the false completion.
- Delete inventoried view generations despite a live pin/hold; the pinned-view residual assertion must fail.
- Lower the new store's schema compatibility floor to 23; the exact schema/floor assertion (schema 24 / floor 24 / supported 23 and exact rendered diagnostic line) must fail. A different old-binary error, including an unsupported authority-witness variant, does not satisfy or mask this mutation.

A failed mutation is oracle evidence only. It does not count as a positive qualification trial.

## Reporting limits

Separate positive supported-profile outcomes, injected interruption cases, and negative-oracle mutations. Preserve every failure, refusal, busy outcome, and incomplete attempt. Report exact copied managed files/tables and hashes, the external source/export residuals, each retained view and its exact unreleased hold, busy/missing/malformed/symlink pin reason, unknown job/legacy-reader limits, and old-binary future-open boundary. Any unreleased named hold or busy pin leaves its whole view as an explicit retryable residual after the fixture-owned hold/reader is released. Malformed, non-regular, symlinked, unreadable, or unknown pin/layout states remain refusal-only residuals without automatic repair. An absent pin is accepted only in the exact durably recorded terminal metadata-only partition: the immutable type-aware view-layout inventory matches, all non-pin file items are deleted and absent, `CURRENT` is durably deleted and absent, that exact pin is durably recorded deleted, remaining directories match the original inventory and have no unclassified contents, and no unknown entries remain. No content may be deleted while its generation has pending or present content without an exclusive pin. The schema-floor refusal does not revoke an already-open v23 reader; while that reader holds the shared generation pin, it may continue querying and purge must preserve the view. Make no claim that raw Git copies, external exports, backups, snapshots, process memory, an already-open legacy reader, or the rest of the host were erased.
